actors: Alice | This app | Agent runner | Lab IdP

step: Alice asks the agent to do something for her
msg: Alice -> This app: ask the agent
note: This app: stores task + trace id
text: Alice is signed in through the Lab IdP, so her session has an access
  token. The app queues a task, remembers her token with it and notes the trace
  id, which will follow the whole job.

step: The agent claims the task as itself
msg: Agent runner -> This app: claim a task
text: The runner uses its own token for this, the same client credentials as
  lab 07. Only an agent acting as itself may claim tasks.

step: The app hands over the task and Alice's token
msg: This app -> Agent runner: task + her token
text: Alice's token is lent for this one task. The agent never sees her
  password or her session, and it can't use her token for anything the IdP
  hasn't agreed to.

step: The agent trades her token for a delegated one
msg: Agent runner -> Lab IdP: exchange tokens
text: This is the token exchange. The runner sends its own credentials and
  Alice's token, and asks for the scopes it needs.

step: The IdP narrows the scopes and names both of them
note: Lab IdP: requested, held, delegable
text: The scopes granted are the overlap of three lists: what the agent asked
  for, what Alice holds, and what this agent is ever trusted with. The new token
  keeps `sub` as Alice and adds `acting_as` for the agent.

step: The delegated token comes back, short-lived
msg: Lab IdP -> Agent runner: delegated token
text: It expires in two minutes, or sooner if Alice's own token does. A
  delegation can't outlive the authority behind it.

step: The agent calls the downstream API for Alice
msg: Agent runner -> This app: read notes + token
text: The tool `read_notes` is marked as delegated, so the runner attaches
  the delegated token. The report it writes afterwards uses the agent's own
  token instead.

step: The API checks that a person and an agent are both in the token
msg: This app -> Lab IdP: POST /introspect
note: This app: accepts delegated only
text: A person's own token has no agent in it. An agent's own token has no
  person in it. Neither belongs here, however valid it is.

step: The audit row names both identities, and the trace ties them together
msg: This app -> Agent runner: Alice's notes
note: This app: audit: agent for alice
text: The row says `agent:tidy-agent` acting `on_behalf_of: idp-alice`, and
  carries the same trace id as the request, the exchange and the read.
